# Vaultwarden safety

## Read-only default

Admin user/org list and non-secret diagnostics are fine when
Vaultwarden work is authorized. Do **not** do any of the following
unless the user gave an **explicit verb this turn naming the object**:

| Class | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| Delete / wipe | user, org, collection, attachments bulk delete |
| Access | disable user, revoke sessions, revoke invites |
| Privilege | promote org-admin, broaden collection access |
| Secrets | rotate/reveal admin token into chat; export vault |
| Irreversible | purge org, reset instance DB, disable 2FA for a user |

Bare “clean up users” / “fix access” without naming targets is **not**
authorization.

## Authorization checklist

Before a gated mutate:

1. Base URL resolved from operator config (not guessed).
2. User/org/collection id listed this turn (or pasted by the user).
3. Explicit verb matches the action (disable ≠ delete; revoke ≠ export).
4. One-sentence risk for deletes and org wipes.
5. Prefer disable / revoke over delete when equivalent.

If any step fails → refuse, say what is missing, and stop.

## Always with refuse-leak

Every mutate still forbids cipher plaintext and admin-token pasting
(`vaultwarden-refuse-leak`). Success receipts are ids + status only.

## Admin token

Rotating `ADMIN_TOKEN` needs an explicit verb. Confirm rotated; tell the
operator to land the new value in their secret store — **never paste
the token**. Update only the store path the user named.

## Aftercare

Re-list the affected user/org and report status. Do not claim success
from a write status alone when a follow-up list is cheap. Still zero
vault secret bytes in the reply.

## Neighbor surfaces

HashiCorp Vault, IdP SSO wiring, and reverse-proxy TLS are out of
scope except as non-secret flags the operator already set.
